# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible: the deploy manifest is
# derived from it.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
MINT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://mint:mint@127.0.0.1:5432/mint

# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Token verification is the only thing this service asks identity for.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# HMAC key for outbound event signatures. Run `openssl rand -base64 48`. The boot guard
# checks the SHAPE — base64 or hex, 32 decoded bytes, a measured entropy floor — so a typed
# value is refused however long it is, and there is no NODE_ENV exemption (micro-org #142).
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# The upstreams. Custody signs, the indexer answers what became of a transaction and what a
# contract now holds, the ledger takes the payment, and pricing says what a dollar costs in EMBER.
CUSTODY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4005
INDEXER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4008
LEDGER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4007
# Required. An order is quoted in US cents and settled in EMBER, and nothing can turn the first
# into the second without a rate — so a deploy cannot be paid for while this is unreachable. That
# coupling is deliberate: you cannot charge somebody in a currency you cannot price, and the only
# alternative to refusing is guessing how much of their money to take (src/pricingclient.ts).
PRICING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4009

# The long-lived credential this service exchanges for the short-lived token it presents to its
# peers. Identity mints for `service:mint` with the scopes this service needs, and each
# peer checks the one it cares about.
#
# THIS REPLACES MINT_SERVICE_TOKEN, which was a TOKEN — and a service token expires in 600
# seconds (identity/src/tokens.ts:28). It was read once at boot and nothing re-minted it, so ten
# minutes into every deployment every call to a peer started failing. A credential does not expire,
# is revocable, confers nothing by itself, and buys a fresh ten-minute token whenever one is
# needed. The ten minutes is unchanged: rotation IS expiry.
#
# `deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh` writes it to compose/estate/tokens.env. It begins `cfsc_`;
# if what you have begins `eyJ` it is a token, not a credential, and the service will refuse it.
#
# Technically optional — the image must be able to boot without one so CI can smoke-test /livez —
# but without it no call to a peer succeeds, and /readyz reports `identity-credential` as a hard
# failure so the replica takes no traffic.
MINT_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
MINT_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# The one network this deployment mints on, and the default for an order that names none.
# MINT_NETWORK=testnet

# `chain -> JSON-RPC endpoint`, as JSON. EMPTY by default, so a chain with no endpoint refuses
# rather than falling back to a public node nobody chose.
#   MINT_RPC_URLS={"ember":"http://127.0.0.1:8545","eth":"https://..."}
# MINT_RPC_URLS={}
# MINT_RPC_DEADLINE_MS=5000
# MINT_UPSTREAM_DEADLINE_MS=5000

# --- the two that cost money if they are wrong -------------------------------------------------

# Subjects permitted to deploy to a MAINNET, comma separated. EMPTY BY DEFAULT, AND EMPTY MEANS
# NOBODY. A mainnet deploy spends the platform's own gas on a contract that then exists for ever at
# an address a customer will publish. The service this replaces gates it on nothing at all.
#   MINT_MAINNET_ALLOWLIST=user:0192...,user:0193...
# MINT_MAINNET_ALLOWLIST=

# Deploys can be turned off without turning the service off, so orders still take payment and the
# queue drains when they are turned back on.
# MINT_DEPLOYS_ENABLED=true

# The gas bounds. NOT tuning knobs: both directions cost money. A floor of zero lets a node quoting
# nothing produce a creation that underbids its own chain and then sits in a mempool being neither
# mined nor refunded; a ceiling that is too high lets a misbehaving node spend a fortune on gas.
# Custody has its own ceiling (MAX_FEE_WEI, 2e18) and refuses past it, so a bound set above that is
# not a wider policy — it is a 403 at the moment of signing, which is a worse way to discover the
# same limit.
# MINT_MIN_GAS_PRICE_WEI=1000000000
# MINT_MAX_GAS_PRICE_WEI=500000000000
# MINT_MAX_FEE_WEI=1000000000000000000

# The price of one deploy, in US CENTS. The order is durable in dollars and settled in EMBER at
# payment time, at the rate PRICING_URL publishes — see src/migrations.ts, migration 6.
#
# 2500 is $25.00, and it is the same price this was before: it read MINT_DEPLOY_PRICE_SHARDS=2500,
# and one Shard is exactly one cent (SHARD has decimals 0, USD is cents, the peg is 100 Shards to
# the dollar), so the re-denomination moved no number. SHARD was retired on 2026-08-04 and the old
# variable is now REFUSED AT BOOT rather than accepted and ignored — a deployment that still sets it
# is stating a price in a unit the estate no longer issues, and silently charging something else
# would leave an operator believing a number that is not the one being charged.
#
# A zero is refused: a free deploy is a free gas bill for anyone who can open an order.
# MINT_DEPLOY_PRICE_USD_CENTS=2500

# How long a deploy may sit unconfirmed before it is called stuck and an operator is told. The
# service this replaces has 180 SECONDS, and it is a REQUEST timeout rather than a stuck deadline.
# MINT_STUCK_MINUTES=30

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by and in tokens.lease_owner. Defaults to the hostname, which
# is the container id under compose and the pod name under Kubernetes — in both cases the thing an
# operator would search for after finding a stuck lease.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process (NODE_OPTIONS=--import @opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register), not by
# src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here, because this service never reads them.
#
# MINT_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test" —
# the database-backed tests truncate.
